---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Hard Problems — Incident Case Studies"
  description="Ten production incidents that shaped engineering judgment. Lock contention at scale, silent failures, architectural incompatibility, database constraints, resilience patterns, leadership under crisis."
>
  <article style="padding: 52px 0;">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <a href="/case-studies" style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none;">← Back to case studies</a>
    </nav>

    <h1 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: var(--fs-32); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 24px;">Production Incidents</h1>

    <p style="font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 32px; max-width: 65ch;">Ten incidents where production pressure exposed a deeper problem. Each follows failure → diagnosis → decision → recovery → lesson.</p>

    <!-- Problem 1 -->
    <section id="incident-01" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">01. Query Shape, Not Tuning</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> 2022</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 3 hours to diagnosis + fix</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Cross-team, blocking</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Paginated queries timing out. Team had exhausted index tuning. Every optimization lever was maxed. Root issue: join happened before pagination. Materializing millions of rows, then limiting to 100, is cardinality inversion.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Reverse operation order: LIMIT from primary table first, then join only for those 100 rows. Changed cardinality from millions-in-join to 100-in-join.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;"><strong>Response time:</strong> 3+ minutes → under 500ms (360x faster). Key insight: separates query tuning from query architecture.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 2 -->
    <section id="incident-02" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">02. MySQL Lock Contention at Scale</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> Early 2022, production cutover</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 1-1.5 days from escalation to fix</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Production-wide regression, all 200+ merchants</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Post-cutover response time spiked from seconds to 5+. Root cause: MySQL LOAD DATA holds locks for the entire operation. With 6 parallel threads per client across 200+ merchants, hundreds of concurrent lock holders. Everything downstream waited for locks.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Reduce parallelism from 6 → 3 concurrent load threads. Released locks sooner. Allowed downstream queries to proceed.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;"><strong>Response time:</strong> returned to baseline. CEO co-owned the recovery communication. Key insight: production scale ≠ staging scale.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 3 -->
    <section id="incident-03" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">03. Real-Time Product Changes in Recommendations</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> 2022-2023</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 3-4 days to build + ship</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Churn driver (merchants seeing stale recommendations)</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Reco (old system) precalculated every 24 hours. If product went out of stock at hour 7, it appeared in recommendations for 17 more hours. Merchants saw broken recommendations, lost trust. CSMs complained (not in sprint planning). It was a churn driver.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Webhook → MySQL landing → Debezium → Pub/Sub. Real-time messages when product updates. Consuming services listen, update blocks in real-time. Max 5-minute lag instead of 24 hours.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Recommendation freshness improved from a 24-hour batch cycle to a maximum five-minute lag for product changes. Key insight: listen to customer-facing teams, not only tickets.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 4 -->
    <section id="incident-04" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">04. MySQL Views → Debezium CDC</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> 2022-2023</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> Phase 1: 2 months (tactical); Phase 2: 1 month (architectural)</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Foundation of data platform architecture</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">MySQL views for normalized serving. Theory: one view, consuming services query it. Reality: views materialize the entire dataset before applying filters. COUNT(*) on one client read millions of rows. Queries: 30+ seconds.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Two-Phase Approach</h3>
        <div style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Phase 1 (Urgent):</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Convert views to CTEs. Apply filters before joins. Bought 2 months. 30s → 1.5-2s.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Phase 2 (Proper Fix):</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Debezium tails MySQL → Pub/Sub. Services normalize into purpose-shaped stores: MongoDB (point lookups), ClickHouse (aggregation). Query denormalized stores directly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Phase 2 achieved same latency as Phase 1 with different approach. Proved design was correct. Became the platform architecture. Key insight: normalize in consumers, not database.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 5 -->
    <section id="incident-05" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">05. Missing Index on Production Query</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> One week after deployment</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 3-4 hours to diagnose + fix</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Intermittent performance degradation</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Consuming service response time spiked. Certain queries slow, others normal. Root: query filtered on MongoDB field without index. Only intermittent because only certain queries hit the unindexed field.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <div style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Immediate:</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Added the index.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Permanent:</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Added query-performance monitoring. If response drops below threshold, alert immediately. Catches regressions in minutes, not days.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Monitoring now prevents similar regressions. Key insight: process gaps matter as much as technical fixes.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 6 -->
    <section id="incident-06" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">06. Real-Time Optimization Gone Wrong</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> December 24, 2025 (Christmas Eve)</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 12 hours troubleshooting</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Production-wide, peak sales, org-wide panic</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Team member optimized recommendation sorting: listen to real-time product changes, update ClickHouse on every change. Didn't understand ClickHouse's memory constraints. With millions of products and real-time traffic, database ran out of memory. Silent failure: reports looked correct but were wrong.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <div style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Immediate:</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Scale ClickHouse RAM to buy breathing room.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
          <p style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">Permanent:</p>
          <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Implement 24-hour batch solution. Had her own the fix (how junior engineers learn). Added AI-assisted code review to flag problematic patterns.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Christmas sales unblocked. Process improved. Team member learned. Key insight: crisis management + mentorship under pressure.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 7 -->
    <section id="incident-07" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">07. Repeated Pattern: Segment Ordering</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> First incident 2023; second incident 2023-2024</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 4 days (first); 4 hours (second)</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Wrong audience got emails</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">First Incident</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">No explicit ORDER BY in segment API. BigQuery returns results in non-guaranteed order. Emails sent to wrong audience. Fixed with explicit ordering.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Second Incident (BQ → ClickHouse Migration)</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Copied query from BigQuery to ClickHouse. Didn't realize ClickHouse's ORDER BY semantics differ (NULL handling, tie-breaking). Same problem: ordering changed. Same customer churned twice.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Systemic Fix</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Three layers: (1) Producer maintains API contract and ordering guarantee. (2) Consumers validate incoming data matches expected ordering before sending emails. (3) Continuous QA validation across all services.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Ordering validated continuously. Key insight: recognize when problems repeat, build systems to prevent it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 8 -->
    <section id="incident-08" style="margin-bottom: 48px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">08. Silent Failure + Algorithmic Insight</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> BQ → ClickHouse migration (2023-2024)</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 2.5 weeks (diagnosis + batched hashing solution)</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Correctness issue (silent failure) + cost issue</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Segment comparison: identify new/removed/unchanged members. On BigQuery: full outer join (expensive but works). Migrated to ClickHouse. Hit memory limits: comparing 100M members requires O(n²) memory. Scaled up RAM. System showed success. But results were silently wrong.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Root Cause</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Trying to move BigQuery's unlimited-memory join model into ClickHouse's tight-memory budget. Incompatibility, not a tuning problem.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution: Batched Hashing with cityHash64</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Instead of comparing all members in one memory-heavy operation: (1) Use cityHash64 to partition both old and new segments deterministically. (2) The same email falls into the same bucket in both datasets. (3) Compare bucket by bucket via hashes. (4) Investigate only buckets whose hashes differ. This kept memory bounded instead of scaling with the full comparison.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;"><strong>Memory:</strong> unlimited → 16GB fixed. <strong>Cost:</strong> ₹3+ lakhs → <sub>₹</sub>50K (6x reduction). <strong>Correctness:</strong> now produces correct results. Key insight: recognize incompatibility, solve algorithmically instead of throwing resources.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 9 -->
    <section id="incident-09" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">09. ClickHouse Materialized-View Scale Mismatch</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> BigQuery → ClickHouse migration (2023–2024)</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> ~2 weeks to diagnose + fix</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Production reports degrading, query latency unacceptable</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">ClickHouse materialized views auto-refresh when underlying tables change. But the materialized-view queries (aggregations + joins) were rescanning large tables at hundreds of events per second. CPU spiked. Reports took minutes to return.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Replaced real-time materialized views with a scheduled hourly batch refresh. Instead of re-aggregating on every single row arrival, precompute aggregations once per hour. Simple: stable CPU, predictable cost, reports under 30 seconds.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Reports stabilized. CPU utilization became predictable. Key insight: database-specific behavior (trigger refresh frequency) matters more than architectural desire for real-time.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 10 -->
    <section id="incident-10" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">10. Node.js Backpressure as a Platform Pattern</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> Early 2024, ongoing data platform growth</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> ~3 weeks to diagnose, design, deploy</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Recurring daily OOM crashes, on-call burden, platform unreliability</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin-bottom: 12px;">Pub/Sub-consuming services (Node.js) hit daily OOMs at a 512MiB pod limit. They were pulling messages as fast as Pub/Sub delivered them, with no regard for available heap. The pod's memory ceiling (not application code) triggered the crash.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Looked like a code-level memory leak — but local testing showed nothing. The failure only surfaced under production traffic volume and real message throughput.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Solution</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Implement backpressure: monitor heap usage in real time, pause message consumption when it climbs to a high water mark (80%), resume once it drops to a safe level (60%). Messages queue safely in Pub/Sub in the meantime. Combine with Kubernetes HPA (horizontal pod autoscaling) for burst traffic.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Result</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin-bottom: 12px;">Crashes went from recurring to essentially eliminated. The pause/resume pattern was adopted across consuming services in the platform.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Key insight: constraints matter more than code. A pod memory limit is real, and feedback loops (pause/resume) beat throwing resources at the symptom.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 11 -->
    <section id="incident-11" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">11. The Over-Engineered Landing Layer</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> Early 2021, post-data platform launch</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> ~3 months to realize, 2 months to simplify</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Operational overhead, team confusion</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">I designed the raw MySQL landing layer with 15 fields per source table, thinking "we need all this context for flexibility." But in practice, we used only 5. The extra 10 fields became operational debt: schema migrations had to touch all 15 fields across 200+ merchants, every change rippled through the system, and team members asked "why do we have this field?" constantly.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">The Mistake</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Optimizing for hypothetical flexibility instead of actual usage patterns. Over-normalized when simplicity would have been better.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">The Fix</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">After 3 months, I simplified the schema down to the essential 5 fields. Migration was painful (had to null out the extra columns safely), but it reduced schema migration time by 40% afterward.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Lesson</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">Start simple, add complexity when evidence demands it. Don't architect for "what if?" — architect for "what is?" and iterate. The cost of over-engineering is ongoing operational pain.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Problem 12 -->
    <section id="incident-12" style="margin-bottom: 56px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 48px;">
      <h2 style="font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 16px;">12. When I Lost the CDC vs Batch Argument</h2>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <div><strong>When:</strong> Late 2021, team discussion on segmentation approach</div>
        <div><strong>Duration:</strong> 1 week of debate, 2 weeks to prove I was wrong</div>
        <div><strong>Impact:</strong> Team morale, technical credibility</div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Problem</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">I was convinced segmentation should use real-time CDC from the landing layer (like everything else). A team member pushed back: "Segmentation updates only daily. We don't need real-time." I thought they were being lazy or didn't understand the architecture.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">The Evidence</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">They ran a 2-week experiment. Real-time segmentation cost 3x more, delivered no user benefit (CSMs only ran campaigns once daily anyway), and added complexity to monitoring. The data proved them right.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">What I Did</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">I admitted I was wrong in the team meeting. We switched to daily batch refresh for segmentation. Simpler, cheaper, same outcome. And importantly, I thanked them for pushing back and for running the experiment.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 12px;">Lesson</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--ink); max-width: 65ch; margin: 0;">The right architecture depends on the actual constraints, not theoretical ideals. Sometimes the "simpler" path is the right path. And team members who challenge you are doing you a favor — listen to them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

